{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.QCasoratianAbel","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.QCasoratianAbel","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.qbessel_casoratian_qabel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.qbessel_casoratian_qabel","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QCasoratianAbel.lean#L78-L92","doc":"**Specialisation to the Hahn–Exton q-Bessel equation `(☆)`.**\n\nWith `a = q^ν` (here the abstract parameter `qpow := q^ν`), `b = −(1 + q^ν − w)`,\n`c = 1`, the Casoratian satisfies `qpow · C₁ = C₀`, i.e. `C(qw) = qpow⁻¹ · C(w)`.\nIterating this first-order `q`-recurrence yields the monomial form\n`C(w) = const · w^{-ν}` that the coincidence propagator `G_q = g₁ g₂ / D` and the\nreduction-of-order second solution rely on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.casoratian_abel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.casoratian_abel","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/QCasoratianAbel.lean#L58-L76","doc":"**q-Casoratian (Abel) law — pointwise form.**\n\nFor a second-order linear `q`-difference equation `a·h₂ + b·h₁ + c·h₀ = 0`\n(values of `h` at `q²w, qw, w`), two solutions `f, g` have Casoratian values\n`C₀ = f₀ g₁ − f₁ g₀` at `w` and `C₁ = f₁ g₂ − f₂ g₁` at `qw` satisfying\n\n    a · C₁ = c · C₀.\n\nThe middle coefficient `b` drops out — only the outer coefficients `a` (at `q²w`)\nand `c` (at `w`) survive, which is what makes the Casoratian a *first-order*\n`q`-recurrence. Proof is the elimination of the `q²w` values via the two\nequations, then `ring`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}